1
00:00:29,396 --> 00:00:31,064
- [gasps]
- [Debbie] Oh.

2
00:00:45,078 --> 00:00:50,083
You have these striking
alabaster cheekbones.

3
00:00:51,151 --> 00:00:52,552
Did you know that?

4
00:00:53,521 --> 00:00:55,054
No. [sighs]

5
00:00:55,055 --> 00:00:56,389
Well, you do.

6
00:00:56,923 --> 00:00:58,391
They're strong.

7
00:00:59,226 --> 00:01:00,093
Like you.

8
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:02,462
You're attractive, Rhonda.

9
00:01:03,029 --> 00:01:05,498
Maybe not
in a conventional sense.

10
00:01:06,166 --> 00:01:10,170
Your beauty
is more severe. [chuckles]

11
00:01:11,104 --> 00:01:15,742
Maybe even feral,
like in a European way.

12
00:01:16,843 --> 00:01:18,178
Yeah.

13
00:01:18,912 --> 00:01:20,546
Let me just...

14
00:01:20,547 --> 00:01:22,249
- Ow!
- Oh...

15
00:01:22,682 --> 00:01:26,052
Hey. It's okay.

16
00:01:26,788 --> 00:01:28,122
We're your friends.

17
00:01:28,890 --> 00:01:32,225
- No one here
is going to hurt you.
- [Debbie] Yeah.

18
00:01:32,226 --> 00:01:34,662
- [Susan] Okay?
- Okay.

19
00:01:35,697 --> 00:01:37,698
[Debbie] Let me just
try one more time.

20
00:01:37,699 --> 00:01:41,569
[♪ "Lady" playing]

21
00:02:16,704 --> 00:02:18,306
[inaudible chatter]

22
00:02:57,712 --> 00:03:00,615
- Oh, Rhonda.
- [chuckling] Oh.

23
00:03:08,790 --> 00:03:11,291
You are stunning.

24
00:03:11,292 --> 00:03:13,829
You're gonna be able to
have sex with anyone you want.

25
00:03:14,262 --> 00:03:16,330
Or anyone in the band,
at least.

26
00:03:27,074 --> 00:03:28,074
[Liz clicks tongue]

27
00:03:28,075 --> 00:03:31,279
She's a fall, not a spring.

28
00:03:32,380 --> 00:03:34,182
She'll need warmer undertones,

29
00:03:34,282 --> 00:03:36,584
but I do like
what you've done with the hair.

30
00:03:36,684 --> 00:03:38,819
I remember when
that homecoming queen crown

31
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:43,291
was placed atop my head
like it was yesterday.

32
00:03:43,691 --> 00:03:45,593
I knew in that moment,

33
00:03:46,127 --> 00:03:49,597
I had to get out
of my shithole town
and make something of myself.

34
00:03:50,164 --> 00:03:53,234
My destiny was sealed
right then and there.

35
00:03:54,535 --> 00:03:58,805
Someone less evolved would have
taken it as a sign to stay put.

36
00:03:58,806 --> 00:04:01,075
Be a big fish in a small pond.

37
00:04:01,542 --> 00:04:04,845
But not me,
I saw a ladder that led out.

38
00:04:04,846 --> 00:04:07,547
I would need to win
Miss Indianapolis next,

39
00:04:07,548 --> 00:04:12,553
and win and win and win
until I got
to where I am today.

40
00:04:14,155 --> 00:04:16,524
A winner's psychology is...

41
00:04:18,025 --> 00:04:19,193
special.

42
00:04:19,493 --> 00:04:20,728
Complex.

43
00:04:21,662 --> 00:04:23,365
Susan knows
what I'm talking about.

44
00:04:23,531 --> 00:04:26,467
And now you do too, Rhonda.

45
00:04:32,406 --> 00:04:33,541
[chuckles]

46
00:04:35,143 --> 00:04:37,477
Did you know my mother
was homecoming queen too?

47
00:04:37,478 --> 00:04:38,613
No.

48
00:04:39,014 --> 00:04:41,950
It's a shame
the royal bloodline
has to die with me.

49
00:04:42,751 --> 00:04:45,319
I'll just have to pretend
you're my daughter, Rhonda.

50
00:04:47,121 --> 00:04:48,222
[Liz sighs]

51
00:04:49,257 --> 00:04:51,692
This must be
what it feels like to adopt.

52
00:04:51,693 --> 00:04:54,295
[inhales deeply] How tragic.

53
00:05:00,568 --> 00:05:02,235
[sniffs]

54
00:05:02,236 --> 00:05:04,805
- Since when
do you wear Giorgio?
- Your father got it for me.

55
00:05:05,406 --> 00:05:08,843
A present for saving your skin
at school the other day.

56
00:05:09,010 --> 00:05:11,412
It's very exciting. It's new.

57
00:05:12,213 --> 00:05:14,682
[♪ suspenseful music plays]

58
00:05:19,186 --> 00:05:20,788
Girlie smell.

59
00:05:23,524 --> 00:05:24,892
[breathing shakily]

60
00:05:25,092 --> 00:05:26,594
Girlie smell.

61
00:05:38,306 --> 00:05:39,607
music plays]

62
00:05:46,414 --> 00:05:48,282
[Bret]
<i>The homecoming count was in.</i>

63
00:05:48,482 --> 00:05:51,385
<i>And even though
it was meant to give us
a rare glimpse of hope,</i>

64
00:05:51,386 --> 00:05:53,153
<i>I couldn't help but feel that</i>

65
00:05:53,154 --> 00:05:56,190
<i>by turning Rhonda's return
into an impossible fantasy,</i>

66
00:05:56,724 --> 00:05:59,528
<i>we'd actually handed
the victory to The Trawler.</i>

67
00:06:00,061 --> 00:06:02,095
The ballots have arrived.

68
00:06:02,096 --> 00:06:03,698
Thank you, Miss Schaffer.

69
00:06:06,668 --> 00:06:08,669
Hi, baby. [kisses]

70
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:15,575
- [Croft] I said thank you,
Miss Schaffer.
- Yeah.

71
00:06:15,576 --> 00:06:17,644
[Croft] Candidates cannot
participate in the tally.

72
00:06:17,645 --> 00:06:20,582
But since you are a female
enrolled as a senior,

73
00:06:20,681 --> 00:06:23,518
you are automatically
considered a candidate.

74
00:06:24,485 --> 00:06:27,759
- Leave those to us.
- But Bret is eligible
to be a candidate.

75
00:06:27,892 --> 00:06:29,249
Why does he get to stay
and count?

76
00:06:29,250 --> 00:06:31,083
Because Bret
has no chance at king.
We all know that.

77
00:06:31,084 --> 00:06:33,020
He's impartial by default.

78
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:34,821
[Debbie sighs]

79
00:06:43,397 --> 00:06:44,897
[door closes]

80
00:06:44,898 --> 00:06:46,399
[Croft sighs]

81
00:06:50,404 --> 00:06:51,871
- [Lydia] Principal.
- [Croft] Thank you.

82
00:06:51,872 --> 00:06:53,273
Well, all right then.

83
00:06:53,274 --> 00:06:54,808
Let's get started.

84
00:06:58,279 --> 00:06:59,813
One for Rhonda.

85
00:07:03,717 --> 00:07:05,352
One for Susan.

86
00:07:06,887 --> 00:07:08,321
One for Rhonda.

87
00:07:10,924 --> 00:07:12,526
One for Susan. [sighs]

88
00:07:19,633 --> 00:07:21,401
[yawning] Oh, my God.

89
00:07:24,538 --> 00:07:26,840
- Hey.
- Is it over? Who won?

90
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:29,642
No, we're still counting.
The halfway mark.

91
00:07:29,643 --> 00:07:33,246
It's so exhausting,
but it's close.

92
00:07:33,513 --> 00:07:36,382
Okay. Just give me
the numbers, Bret.
I run a charity.

93
00:07:36,383 --> 00:07:38,685
I need facts
that I can work with, okay?

94
00:07:38,819 --> 00:07:40,553
- It's looking good for Rhonda.
- Mm-hmm.

95
00:07:40,554 --> 00:07:42,488
But honestly,
it's too close to call it.

96
00:07:42,489 --> 00:07:43,957
- It's neck and neck.
- [sighs]

97
00:07:44,691 --> 00:07:45,593
Okay.

98
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:48,862
What about me and Susan?
Who's ahead there?

99
00:07:49,262 --> 00:07:50,597
What does that matter?

100
00:07:51,231 --> 00:07:54,401
Rhonda's in the lead.
Susan's right behind
Rhonda, okay?

101
00:07:55,769 --> 00:07:56,970
And me?

102
00:07:58,004 --> 00:07:59,706
You have one vote.

103
00:08:04,512 --> 00:08:08,415
Aw. Debbie,
that make you upset?

104
00:08:09,449 --> 00:08:13,019
[stammers]
I didn't think you cared.
You're not in it to win.

105
00:08:13,020 --> 00:08:14,221
I know.

106
00:08:14,821 --> 00:08:17,691
I was never going to win
because I never do.

107
00:08:18,625 --> 00:08:19,993
Susan does.

108
00:08:21,261 --> 00:08:22,529
[sighs]

109
00:08:24,364 --> 00:08:30,270
This entire school thinks
that I'm a frivolous
and spiteful clown.

110
00:08:31,238 --> 00:08:32,705
[scoffs]

111
00:08:32,706 --> 00:08:35,642
All the charity in the world
can't change how people see me.

112
00:08:40,814 --> 00:08:42,682
I don't see you like that.

113
00:08:47,321 --> 00:08:50,924
Would it make you
feel any better if I told you
that one vote was from me?

114
00:08:54,494 --> 00:08:55,595
No.

115
00:08:57,998 --> 00:09:00,434
Because it would
be a lie, Bret.

116
00:09:02,769 --> 00:09:03,769
[sniffs]

117
00:09:03,770 --> 00:09:05,472
I was that one vote.

118
00:09:07,507 --> 00:09:09,776
[voice breaking]
I voted for myself.

119
00:09:10,410 --> 00:09:12,245
We're still counting.

120
00:09:16,984 --> 00:09:18,385
[sighs]

121
00:09:23,024 --> 00:09:26,460
[laughing] Mmm.

122
00:09:27,027 --> 00:09:28,162
[Croft clears throat]

123
00:09:28,728 --> 00:09:31,698
Thank you, Mr. Ellis,
for joining us.
Shall we resume?

124
00:09:36,303 --> 00:09:38,371
- Thank you, Lydia.
- [Lydia] Mm-hmm.

125
00:09:46,013 --> 00:09:47,380
One for Debbie.

126
00:09:53,386 --> 00:09:54,821
One for Debbie.

127
00:09:56,389 --> 00:09:57,491
Debbie.

128
00:09:59,593 --> 00:10:00,761
Debbie.

129
00:10:03,997 --> 00:10:05,532
Attention, students.

130
00:10:05,533 --> 00:10:07,268
I want to quickly
interrupt your school day

131
00:10:07,269 --> 00:10:10,305
to announce
this year's official
senior homecoming court

132
00:10:10,505 --> 00:10:13,740
in advance
of the final crowning
of homecoming king and queen.

133
00:10:13,741 --> 00:10:18,478
<i>Your 1981 homecoming court
belongs</i> to...

134
00:10:18,479 --> 00:10:20,414
<i>- Rhonda Fox.</i>
- [gasps]

135
00:10:25,887 --> 00:10:27,288
<i>Susan Reynolds.</i>

136
00:10:27,455 --> 00:10:29,557
[students cheering, applause]

137
00:10:29,757 --> 00:10:31,193
<i>Debbie Schaffer.</i>

138
00:10:31,359 --> 00:10:33,393
- [gasps]
- [applause]

139
00:10:33,394 --> 00:10:35,730
<i>- Thom Wright.</i>
- [whooping, applause]

140
00:10:37,165 --> 00:10:39,867
- <i>Ryan Gifford.</i>
- [applause]

141
00:10:42,303 --> 00:10:44,238
<i>And Robert Mallory.</i>

142
00:10:45,339 --> 00:10:46,941
Thank you. Thank you.

143
00:10:46,942 --> 00:10:49,877
Congratulations to
this year's homecoming court.

144
00:10:49,878 --> 00:10:51,345
[sighs]

145
00:10:51,346 --> 00:10:54,649
<i>We look forward to crowning
the king and queen.</i>

146
00:11:11,332 --> 00:11:12,834
Well, look who it is.

147
00:11:15,470 --> 00:11:19,729
- That's quite an outfit.
- They say to dress
for the job you want.

148
00:11:19,895 --> 00:11:22,698
- Yeah, what job is that?
- King.

149
00:11:23,566 --> 00:11:25,700
Come on, I got you something.

150
00:11:25,701 --> 00:11:26,769
Come on.

151
00:11:30,873 --> 00:11:33,509
You know, I thought you
didn't care about that stuff.

152
00:11:33,609 --> 00:11:36,111
I don't, but I do care
about Thom losing.

153
00:11:36,745 --> 00:11:37,914
[Susan] Mm-hmm.

154
00:11:38,348 --> 00:11:40,615
Listen, if I do
become homecoming king,

155
00:11:40,616 --> 00:11:43,519
I want you to know that
Rhonda can never be my queen.

156
00:11:45,822 --> 00:11:47,022
You're my queen.

157
00:11:47,756 --> 00:11:49,058
Robert, stop.

158
00:11:49,959 --> 00:11:51,093
We can't.

159
00:12:05,541 --> 00:12:08,977
- [chuckling] Robert,
Giorgio is not cheap.
- [chuckles]

160
00:12:08,978 --> 00:12:10,512
Neither are you.

161
00:12:19,989 --> 00:12:21,491
[sniffs]

162
00:12:21,891 --> 00:12:24,760
Oh, it's wonderful.

163
00:12:27,363 --> 00:12:28,563
What are you doing?

164
00:12:28,564 --> 00:12:30,699
I want to smell like my girl.

165
00:12:35,738 --> 00:12:37,506
[♪ upbeat music playing]

166
00:12:37,507 --> 00:12:41,977
[announcer] Everyone,
please welcome
your freshman class float.

167
00:12:41,978 --> 00:12:44,747
- The Jaws of Opportunity.
[chuckles]
- [cheering]

168
00:12:44,847 --> 00:12:47,850
They sure won't get swallowed
by the pressure.

169
00:12:50,052 --> 00:12:51,152
All right.

170
00:12:51,153 --> 00:12:52,922
Next up, we have
the sophomores'

171
00:12:53,055 --> 00:12:57,125
Setting Sail
to New Destinations.

172
00:12:57,126 --> 00:12:58,494
Excuse me. Thank you.

173
00:12:58,727 --> 00:12:59,962
[horn honking]

174
00:12:59,963 --> 00:13:01,130
Thanks so much.

175
00:13:01,897 --> 00:13:06,235
Believe it or not,
the concession stand
wouldn't make you a daiquiri,

176
00:13:06,236 --> 00:13:08,871
so here you go.

177
00:13:09,772 --> 00:13:11,974
It's Tab with a half a flask
of rye bourbon.

178
00:13:11,975 --> 00:13:13,676
[announcer]
Next, we have the junior class
with their float...

179
00:13:13,876 --> 00:13:15,644
Nearly the same thing.

180
00:13:15,645 --> 00:13:17,745
- [announcer]
Give My Regards to Broadway.
- [cheering, applause]

181
00:13:17,746 --> 00:13:21,384
If that doesn't
razzle-dazzle you,
I don't know what does.

182
00:13:21,517 --> 00:13:24,018
[scoffs]
Look at this god-awful float.

183
00:13:24,019 --> 00:13:25,820
[chuckles]

184
00:13:25,821 --> 00:13:28,524
Oh, you are about to
put the whole school
to shame, Steven.

185
00:13:28,691 --> 00:13:30,393
- Yeah.
- I'm proud of you.

186
00:13:30,893 --> 00:13:35,764
And last up,
we have the senior class
with their float.

187
00:13:38,033 --> 00:13:42,637
"The Last Fantasia,
a deconstructed opera

188
00:13:42,638 --> 00:13:48,911
wherein aesthetics and context
refuse the choke hold
of expectation,

189
00:13:49,011 --> 00:13:54,984
heralding a 1980s
defined by mutiny of form."

190
00:13:56,118 --> 00:13:58,387
- [gasps]
- [all cheering]

191
00:14:02,858 --> 00:14:04,393
[student] Good job, guys!

192
00:14:09,698 --> 00:14:11,435
It's a masterpiece.

193
00:14:11,535 --> 00:14:13,470
You know, in your 12 years
of working for me,

194
00:14:13,570 --> 00:14:16,173
I've never seen you
do one single thing right,

195
00:14:16,773 --> 00:14:19,543
- but tonight, you came close.
- [sighs]

196
00:14:19,676 --> 00:14:21,844
I just want us
to remember this...

197
00:14:21,845 --> 00:14:23,913
[inhales deeply]
...this moment.

198
00:14:23,914 --> 00:14:25,682
[announcer]
All right, everyone,
that does it for our parade...

199
00:14:25,683 --> 00:14:27,384
How did Debbie pull this off?

200
00:14:27,517 --> 00:14:29,051
[announcer] Next up
will be your very own...

201
00:14:29,052 --> 00:14:31,122
Maybe some of me
rubbed off on her after all.

202
00:14:31,321 --> 00:14:35,825
...announcing the winners
of this year's
homecoming king and queen.

203
00:14:38,995 --> 00:14:40,230
I don't know.

204
00:14:40,463 --> 00:14:41,732
Float's weird.

205
00:14:41,865 --> 00:14:43,799
Am I the only one
who doesn't get it?

206
00:14:43,800 --> 00:14:47,971
I think it's visionary.
Ferdinando Scarfiotti's
a genius.

207
00:14:59,015 --> 00:15:00,551
Don't be nervous, Rhonda.

208
00:15:00,650 --> 00:15:03,319
This is yours to win.
Just try to breathe.

209
00:15:03,320 --> 00:15:06,923
And now, the moment
we've all been waiting for.

210
00:15:06,924 --> 00:15:11,093
I'd like to welcome this year's
homecoming court to the stage.

211
00:15:11,094 --> 00:15:12,762
[cheering]

212
00:15:27,811 --> 00:15:29,346
Let's go, Debbie!

213
00:15:29,579 --> 00:15:34,351
Here are your final nominees
for homecoming queen and king.

214
00:15:34,584 --> 00:15:40,390
And with no further ado,
your homecoming queen
this year is...

215
00:15:40,624 --> 00:15:44,394
[drumroll]

216
00:15:47,564 --> 00:15:48,498
Susan Reynolds.

217
00:15:48,499 --> 00:15:49,966
[crowd cheering]

218
00:15:55,839 --> 00:15:57,774
[chuckling]

219
00:16:00,644 --> 00:16:03,879
Fuck me raw. [sighs]

220
00:16:03,880 --> 00:16:05,382
Fuck.

221
00:16:08,451 --> 00:16:10,986
[Croft] And your
homecoming king goes to...

222
00:16:10,987 --> 00:16:12,255
Robert Mallory.

223
00:16:12,489 --> 00:16:14,558
[cheering]

224
00:16:16,826 --> 00:16:18,929
[Robert] Guess they like
the new kid, huh?

225
00:16:27,571 --> 00:16:28,772
Um...

226
00:16:29,539 --> 00:16:32,141
Thank you all so much.

227
00:16:32,142 --> 00:16:35,679
This means the world to me.
It really does.

228
00:16:37,380 --> 00:16:39,114
But I can't accept this crown.

229
00:16:39,115 --> 00:16:41,851
- [crowd gasping, muttering]
- It belongs to a true queen,

230
00:16:41,852 --> 00:16:43,686
a survivor,

231
00:16:43,687 --> 00:16:45,756
- and a beauty in her own right.
- [whispers]
She's gonna give it to Debbie.

232
00:16:45,889 --> 00:16:50,226
I'd like to give this
to Rhonda Fox.

233
00:16:50,227 --> 00:16:52,062
[cheering, applause]

234
00:16:52,963 --> 00:16:56,533
- [sighs]
- [chuckling]

235
00:16:57,901 --> 00:16:59,235
[chuckles]

236
00:16:59,402 --> 00:17:01,738
Come on, Rhonda.
You deserve it.

237
00:17:01,905 --> 00:17:03,974
[cheering, applause continues]

238
00:17:40,877 --> 00:17:42,512
[sniffs]

239
00:18:01,064 --> 00:18:03,999
- Susan Reynolds kidnapped me.
- What?

240
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:05,169
[crowd gasps]

241
00:18:05,368 --> 00:18:07,904
[♪ dramatic music plays]

242
00:18:09,005 --> 00:18:11,241
- That's not true.
- [feedback screeches]

243
00:18:11,242 --> 00:18:12,576
[Rhonda gagging]

244
00:18:13,476 --> 00:18:15,946
That's not true.
Rhonda, why would you say that?

245
00:18:42,839 --> 00:18:44,774
[sobbing]

246
00:19:07,965 --> 00:19:09,733
What are you
searching for, Bret?

247
00:19:11,735 --> 00:19:13,569
A police contact.
I need to help Susan.

248
00:19:13,570 --> 00:19:15,706
I wouldn't worry about Susan.
She has an alibi.

249
00:19:15,839 --> 00:19:19,108
We were at my house
both of the nights that Rhonda
